WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.278 --> 00:00:03.233
An analysis can inform a decision, but it does

2
00:00:03.256 --> 00:00:06.478
not make the decision by itself. Before putting a

3
00:00:06.500 --> 00:00:09.722
change into practice, confirm what has been authorized, by

4
00:00:09.744 --> 00:00:13.611
whom, under which requirements and with what resources. If

5
00:00:13.633 --> 00:00:16.567
the status is unclear, pause and ask the responsible

6
00:00:16.589 --> 00:00:17.211
office.

7
00:00:19.099 --> 00:00:23.343
For our reminder example, imagine the responsible people have

8
00:00:23.366 --> 00:00:27.410
reviewed the options and authorized a particular approach. The

9
00:00:27.432 --> 00:00:30.921
exercise does not assume that text-only reminders were approved

10
00:00:30.943 --> 00:00:33.899
or that a real program has changed its process.

11
00:00:34.366 --> 00:00:36.254
Your task is to plan the work that would

12
00:00:36.277 --> 00:00:38.743
be needed for the approach actually authorized.

13
00:00:39.474 --> 00:00:42.363
For your own example, identify what you may do

14
00:00:42.386 --> 00:00:45.763
within your role, what requires coordination and what another

15
00:00:45.786 --> 00:00:49.741
person or office must decide. Use the applicable implementation

16
00:00:49.763 --> 00:00:53.097
process rather than treating the learning activity as approval.

17
00:00:54.286 --> 00:00:56.930
Write down the steps that bring the authorized change

18
00:00:56.952 --> 00:01:00.974
into everyday use. Who prepares the information? Who checks

19
00:01:00.997 --> 00:01:06.019
required wording, accessibility, language and privacy? Who maintains contact

20
00:01:06.041 --> 00:01:09.486
information through approved channels? Who responds when a reminder

21
00:01:09.508 --> 00:01:11.908
is not received or a person needs help?

22
00:01:12.563 --> 00:01:16.741
Also ask what happens at the handoff. If a

23
00:01:16.763 --> 00:01:19.830
message directs someone to call a number, the people

24
00:01:19.852 --> 00:01:23.474
answering need to know the purpose, available options and

25
00:01:23.497 --> 00:01:26.319
where to route a question. A change is not

26
00:01:26.341 --> 00:01:29.163
fully prepared if its next step is unclear.

27
00:01:29.897 --> 00:01:34.319
Use roles and proposed responsibilities without inventing commitments. If

28
00:01:34.341 --> 00:01:37.497
a resource, reviewer or deadline has not been confirmed,

29
00:01:37.674 --> 00:01:40.186
record it as pending and identify who can resolve

30
00:01:40.208 --> 00:01:40.541
it.

31
00:01:41.774 --> 00:01:45.041
Explain what is changing, what stays the same and

32
00:01:45.063 --> 00:01:49.019
what people can do next. Consider accessible formats, understandable

33
00:01:49.041 --> 00:01:52.463
wording, appropriate language support and a way to ask

34
00:01:52.486 --> 00:01:56.552
questions. Confirm those arrangements with the responsible people and

35
00:01:56.574 --> 00:01:57.752
use current requirements.

36
00:01:58.452 --> 00:02:01.697
The method used to communicate about a change should

37
00:02:01.719 --> 00:02:04.719
not quietly recreate the barrier the change was meant

38
00:02:04.741 --> 00:02:08.497
to address. For example, if access to text messages

39
00:02:08.519 --> 00:02:11.919
is uncertain, a text-only announcement about the new reminder

40
00:02:11.941 --> 00:02:15.186
process may leave some people without a usable explanation.

41
00:02:16.057 --> 00:02:18.679
Do not promise that every person will have the

42
00:02:18.701 --> 00:02:22.879
same experience. Check whether the communication and assistance route

43
00:02:22.901 --> 00:02:26.346
work in practice, and provide a responsible path for

44
00:02:26.368 --> 00:02:27.368
raising a concern.

45
00:02:28.868 --> 00:02:32.312
Every option needs a plan for exceptions and maintenance.

46
00:02:33.112 --> 00:02:35.979
What happens if a message fails, a contact method

47
00:02:36.001 --> 00:02:39.979
changes, an interpreter or accessible format is needed, or

48
00:02:40.001 --> 00:02:43.446
a person asks to use another available route? Identify

49
00:02:43.468 --> 00:02:46.468
the appropriate process instead of promising an outcome you

50
00:02:46.490 --> 00:02:47.423
cannot authorize.

51
00:02:48.212 --> 00:02:51.723
Make sure someone is responsible for keeping the information

52
00:02:51.746 --> 00:02:55.146
current. If a notice is revised, who updates its

53
00:02:55.168 --> 00:02:58.146
other approved formats and checks that the help route

54
00:02:58.168 --> 00:03:02.034
still works? If a handoff changes, who makes sure

55
00:03:02.057 --> 00:03:04.946
the next person has the information needed to respond?

56
00:03:05.676 --> 00:03:09.609
Keep private records in their approved locations. Learning notes

57
00:03:09.631 --> 00:03:13.720
should contain no names, case details, health information or

58
00:03:13.742 --> 00:03:17.964
personnel records. A general implementation plan can identify responsible

59
00:03:17.987 --> 00:03:20.609
roles without reproducing confidential information.

60
00:03:21.820 --> 00:03:24.909
In the activity below, consider the team preparing an

61
00:03:24.931 --> 00:03:28.753
authorized reminder change. Choose the response that best supports

62
00:03:28.776 --> 00:03:32.131
people using the process, then read the feedback. You

63
00:03:32.153 --> 00:03:35.176
can compare the choices without recording a personal experience.

64
00:03:35.969 --> 00:03:39.058
For your own example, write one action within your

65
00:03:39.080 --> 00:03:42.769
role, one handoff that needs coordination and one unresolved

66
00:03:42.791 --> 00:03:46.413
question. If a decision is still pending, record the

67
00:03:46.436 --> 00:03:49.791
review needed before implementation rather than treating the proposal

68
00:03:49.813 --> 00:03:50.747
as authorized.

69
00:03:51.936 --> 00:03:55.980
Implementation is not the end of the decision. In

70
00:03:56.002 --> 00:03:59.247
the final lesson, we will distinguish a completed activity

71
00:03:59.269 --> 00:04:02.691
from evidence of a useful result and identify who

72
00:04:02.713 --> 00:04:05.269
will review what happened and what may need to

73
00:04:05.291 --> 00:04:05.736
change.
